John McCain almost lost his bid to  become the Republican nominee in 2007  based on the fact that he had for a long  time support comprehensive immigration  reform or as it was known back then as  McCain Kennedy in two thousand six and  two thousand seven when he was preparing  his run for presidency he tried to  offload the heavy lifting of  comprehensive immigration reform to his  colleague in the Senate from Arizona jon  kyl who had just won reelection on a  largely anti-immigration campaign  something that people in arizona noticed  but not many people outside of arizona  for mccain is a very interesting issue  part of it stems from his kind of elite  idea that he thinks that the grass root  of his own Republican Party tend to be  kind of knuckle draggers 10 to flirt  with racism and xenophobia that he finds  personally abhorrent one of his best  friends the Naval Academy whose guy  named Frank gambella was a  mexican-american who became referred to  as the mex and ever since then he has  always spoken very movingly about all  these sort of Spanish surnames that he  finds in the Vietnam War Memorial he has  an allergy to racism that I think is is  heartening on the other hand like all of  McCain's reforms his ideas about what to  do about immigration to make a grand  compromise end up having deleterious  effects on individual liberty largely  because his schemes would basically end  up forcing people to use the e-verify  program it would force a very a  technocratic and bureaucratic way for  employers to line up with immigrants  coming from abroad and by the end the  bill that got killed was not something  that people who are in favor of a  streamlined immigration process I think  could really support but what you will  find is that since he has now come out  against his own bill and even little  parts of it including the DREAM Act  which would have given college tuition  to illegal immigrants in-state tuition  McCain is distancing himself from that  low point of his candidacy and you won't  hear him talk certainly this week  about immigration which you will hear on  the Democratic side is that if there's a  single person running for president this  time around who has the greatest chance  to do something about immigration once  he's elected it's not Barack Obama it's  John McCain
 

